WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.829 --> 00:00:05.870
MATT FEIGAL: I'd like to
take a quick temperature

00:00:05.870 --> 00:00:07.790
check of the audience.

00:00:07.790 --> 00:00:09.800
Who is running
what you would call

00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:13.724
microservices in production
today at your company?

00:00:13.724 --> 00:00:14.390
A lot of people.

00:00:14.390 --> 00:00:15.260
OK, good.

00:00:15.260 --> 00:00:18.980
Who is running more
than 60 of them

00:00:18.980 --> 00:00:21.690
in parallel at
your company today?

00:00:21.690 --> 00:00:22.780
OK, fewer.

00:00:22.780 --> 00:00:25.340
There are some challenges
still to overcome

00:00:25.340 --> 00:00:26.500
in getting up this road.

00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:28.333
And a lot of you didn't
even raise your hand

00:00:28.333 --> 00:00:30.470
because you're not running
them in production.

00:00:30.470 --> 00:00:33.950
Pay attention, then, because
as you make this journey,

00:00:33.950 --> 00:00:38.270
you're going to need this
information pretty soon.

00:00:38.270 --> 00:00:39.980
The structure of
our talk today--

00:00:39.980 --> 00:00:43.040
first, I'm going to
talk about this journey

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:46.430
to cloud native microservices,
and why it's a great place

00:00:46.430 --> 00:00:49.220
to make them resilient and
make your developers focusing

00:00:49.220 --> 00:00:51.530
on the pieces you want them to.

00:00:51.530 --> 00:00:54.650
Jean from Blendle will talk
about his experience of this,

00:00:54.650 --> 00:00:57.260
and his team's experience going
through exactly this story

00:00:57.260 --> 00:00:58.220
at Blendle.

00:00:58.220 --> 00:01:00.260
And then in the second
half, after Jean,

00:01:00.260 --> 00:01:02.480
I will go into a bit
more technical details

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:06.910
about microservices
on Kubernetes.

00:01:06.910 --> 00:01:07.880
[PHONE RINGING]

00:01:07.880 --> 00:01:09.330
I don't think that's my phone.

00:01:09.330 --> 00:01:12.080
OK, good.

00:01:12.080 --> 00:01:15.890
We are a little behind schedule,
so we will speed up, accelerate

00:01:15.890 --> 00:01:17.240
through some pieces a bit.

00:01:17.240 --> 00:01:20.270
If we skip over some stuff
that you really wish you knew,

00:01:20.270 --> 00:01:22.454
that you think is the most
important part, sorry.

00:01:22.454 --> 00:01:24.620
We will be hanging out here
at the end of the stage.

00:01:24.620 --> 00:01:26.510
Please come up and just ask
us, hey, what about that?

00:01:26.510 --> 00:01:28.760
You skipped over that too
fast, I didn't understand.

00:01:28.760 --> 00:01:30.920
We'd like to share.

00:01:30.920 --> 00:01:33.260
So from a background, I
am a software developer

00:01:33.260 --> 00:01:34.020
by background.

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:35.520
I spent a lot of years doing it.

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:37.050
I love it!

00:01:37.050 --> 00:01:39.030
But the challenges can be hard.

00:01:39.030 --> 00:01:42.380
And these days, it's not
really getting any easier

00:01:42.380 --> 00:01:45.140
to be a software developer.

00:01:45.140 --> 00:01:47.090
So in the old days--

00:01:47.090 --> 00:01:50.030
the good old days, we
like to imagine them--

00:01:50.030 --> 00:01:52.550
this was all I had to
worry about, right?

00:01:52.550 --> 00:01:55.010
I would write my code,
I would compile it.

00:01:55.010 --> 00:01:57.860
I would ship it to the
users, they would install it.

00:01:57.860 --> 00:02:00.320
They would call me with some
annoying support questions

00:02:00.320 --> 00:02:03.620
sometimes, usually a PEBKAC
error or something like that.

00:02:03.620 --> 00:02:05.990
And then we would
write a new version,

00:02:05.990 --> 00:02:08.245
compile it, ship
it to them again.

00:02:08.245 --> 00:02:09.320
That was it.

00:02:09.320 --> 00:02:10.050
That was simple.

00:02:10.050 --> 00:02:13.440
I could focus on this business
problem that my product owner--

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:15.230
we didn't call them
that back then--

00:02:15.230 --> 00:02:16.610
that my business
needed to solve,

00:02:16.610 --> 00:02:19.710
or my customer needed to solve,
that would make us more money.

00:02:19.710 --> 00:02:22.636
And then things started to
get a little more complicated.

00:02:22.636 --> 00:02:24.260
But they're still
pretty simple, right?

00:02:24.260 --> 00:02:26.450
We had a client--

00:02:26.450 --> 00:02:28.880
that's a Palm Pilot,
not an Android,

00:02:28.880 --> 00:02:32.420
so you know how old I am.

00:02:32.420 --> 00:02:36.350
We had a Java back-end
where we stuck everything--

00:02:36.350 --> 00:02:39.830
the authentication, inventory
management rules, a logging

00:02:39.830 --> 00:02:40.880
library.

00:02:40.880 --> 00:02:44.180
It was probably just
one server back then.

00:02:44.180 --> 00:02:46.725
Or one time, it was my laptop,
because we couldn't deploy it

00:02:46.725 --> 00:02:47.600
into the server room.

00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:51.050
So we stuck my laptop into the
data center with a note saying,

00:02:51.050 --> 00:02:54.380
"Don't unplug this, please."

00:02:54.380 --> 00:02:56.720
So it's not necessarily
the good old days.

00:02:56.720 --> 00:02:58.520
There was a database
managed by our DBA.

00:02:58.520 --> 00:03:00.920
She would really help us
out with tuning the index

00:03:00.920 --> 00:03:02.040
and planning this stuff.

00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:05.270
But we had to all work together.

00:03:05.270 --> 00:03:07.850
And we thought a lot about
software architecture--

00:03:07.850 --> 00:03:10.400
patterns, best
practices-- but it

00:03:10.400 --> 00:03:14.700
was still three monoliths that
were tightly coupled together.

00:03:14.700 --> 00:03:18.230
Which is actually-- we'd
still ship and compile them

00:03:18.230 --> 00:03:20.150
on all big chunks,
but on a rollout,

00:03:20.150 --> 00:03:21.780
we have to coordinate
with downtime.

00:03:21.780 --> 00:03:25.760
And we really actually had
one scattered monolith,

00:03:25.760 --> 00:03:29.540
which, we made lives a
lot harder for ourselves.

00:03:29.540 --> 00:03:30.950
Fast forward many, many years.

00:03:30.950 --> 00:03:31.790
A lot has happened.

00:03:31.790 --> 00:03:34.280
The next stage in that journey
is this explosive growth

00:03:34.280 --> 00:03:36.674
of the internet,
new technologies.

00:03:36.674 --> 00:03:38.840
World Press Photos was
talking to you about the fact

00:03:38.840 --> 00:03:41.510
that times are
changing, companies

00:03:41.510 --> 00:03:45.630
need to move faster because
the environment's faster.

00:03:45.630 --> 00:03:48.560
So we start decomposing
our scattered monoliths

00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:50.720
into microservices that
we can test and deploy

00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:53.630
individually and faster,
keep functionality alive.

00:03:53.630 --> 00:03:54.920
That's microservices.

00:03:54.920 --> 00:03:57.680
We build up automation
services for better deployments

00:03:57.680 --> 00:03:59.365
to keep these systems alive.

00:03:59.365 --> 00:04:00.740
And our modern
applications start

00:04:00.740 --> 00:04:02.860
to look a little bit like this.

00:04:02.860 --> 00:04:03.694
Oh!

00:04:03.694 --> 00:04:04.610
That's not three-tier.

00:04:04.610 --> 00:04:07.040
That's a whole bunch of stuff.

00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:08.990
We've got it
separated across zones

00:04:08.990 --> 00:04:11.210
so that if one whole zone
dies, it stays alive.

00:04:11.210 --> 00:04:13.760
We keep serving to our users.

00:04:13.760 --> 00:04:15.384
Ooh, but now that
they're separated,

00:04:15.384 --> 00:04:17.300
we need to load balance
between those machines

00:04:17.300 --> 00:04:19.850
and those machines, and we
need to have coordination

00:04:19.850 --> 00:04:21.890
of state between them
so we have task queues--

00:04:21.890 --> 00:04:24.860
and oh, wait, health checks
that need to be coordinated.

00:04:24.860 --> 00:04:27.800
And wow, it just
got complicated.

00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:30.140
Because what I want as a
developer to care about

00:04:30.140 --> 00:04:33.537
is this-- this little
piece, this red piece.

00:04:33.537 --> 00:04:35.120
My product owner
wants me to build it,

00:04:35.120 --> 00:04:37.119
I need to build it to
make money for my business

00:04:37.119 --> 00:04:38.480
or my customers.

00:04:38.480 --> 00:04:40.940
I want to knock a new feature
out the door every week.

00:04:40.940 --> 00:04:45.290
But in order to do that, I
have to understand all of this.

00:04:45.290 --> 00:04:47.314
In order for my
application to live here,

00:04:47.314 --> 00:04:48.980
I've got to understand
what's happening.

00:04:48.980 --> 00:04:51.860
I can't just write my Java
code or write my Python code

00:04:51.860 --> 00:04:53.870
and trust it's going to go well.

00:04:53.870 --> 00:04:56.480
And not just me, and
not just my teams,

00:04:56.480 --> 00:04:59.330
but each of the 20
other deployment

00:04:59.330 --> 00:05:02.330
teams running the 60 other
microservices we have to have.

00:05:02.330 --> 00:05:04.360
All have to have
the same picture,

00:05:04.360 --> 00:05:06.450
use their head cycles
understanding this,

00:05:06.450 --> 00:05:08.620
stay consistent
about how they do it.

00:05:08.620 --> 00:05:12.030
Otherwise, you're in trouble.

00:05:12.030 --> 00:05:14.040
And pieces of this
infrastructure

00:05:14.040 --> 00:05:16.150
start creeping into my code.

00:05:16.150 --> 00:05:17.957
How do I handle
backoff requests?

00:05:17.957 --> 00:05:20.040
How do I configure-- how
do I find those services?

00:05:20.040 --> 00:05:22.290
Stuff that I shouldn't have
to write into my code that

00:05:22.290 --> 00:05:24.960
makes it harder to operate
my own code because

00:05:24.960 --> 00:05:26.070
of this infrastructure.

00:05:26.070 --> 00:05:28.620
My application shouldn't care
that it's running in this.

00:05:28.620 --> 00:05:31.140
It should just run,
and the environment

00:05:31.140 --> 00:05:33.420
should take care of it.

00:05:33.420 --> 00:05:35.520
And this is without
even touching

00:05:35.520 --> 00:05:37.920
on these many other
complicated parts of it that

00:05:37.920 --> 00:05:40.440
start creeping into my code.

00:05:40.440 --> 00:05:42.690
My team might be using a
completely different tooling

00:05:42.690 --> 00:05:45.070
than that other team, because
they evaluated their problem,

00:05:45.070 --> 00:05:46.080
and they said, you know what?

00:05:46.080 --> 00:05:47.770
Go is the right
thing for us to use.

00:05:47.770 --> 00:05:49.187
We need high-streaming
throughput.

00:05:49.187 --> 00:05:50.561
Another team says,
you know what?

00:05:50.561 --> 00:05:51.960
.NET is the right
thing for this.

00:05:51.960 --> 00:05:53.880
My team says no, we love Python.

00:05:53.880 --> 00:05:56.320
There's no good reason for
it, just religious reasons--

00:05:56.320 --> 00:05:57.570
and that's happened sometimes.

00:05:57.570 --> 00:05:59.410
But there's usually
a good reason.

00:05:59.410 --> 00:06:02.130
So in the interest of time, I'm
going to skip all over these.

00:06:02.130 --> 00:06:04.680
I think you all recognize these.

00:06:04.680 --> 00:06:07.740
What I really want to
do is write some code

00:06:07.740 --> 00:06:12.067
to solve a business problem
to make it fun for me,

00:06:12.067 --> 00:06:12.900
because I like that.

00:06:12.900 --> 00:06:15.840
I like knocking a new
feature out every week

00:06:15.840 --> 00:06:19.020
to create a new business
opportunity, to make money

00:06:19.020 --> 00:06:21.700
for my customers, or whatever.

00:06:21.700 --> 00:06:24.592
So what happened?

00:06:24.592 --> 00:06:26.550
It's not the writing of
the code that got hard.

00:06:26.550 --> 00:06:29.700
That's still fun, knocking
out these problems every week.

00:06:29.700 --> 00:06:32.370
Running that code in
production, keeping

00:06:32.370 --> 00:06:35.490
it alive with the requirements
of the crazy modern times

00:06:35.490 --> 00:06:37.900
is creating all sorts of
complexity for our teams.

00:06:37.900 --> 00:06:39.990
So in the good old days,
we'd write our code,

00:06:39.990 --> 00:06:41.680
we'd pass it to the
QA team for testing.

00:06:41.680 --> 00:06:43.510
They'd send it back
to us five times.

00:06:43.510 --> 00:06:45.620
We'd give it to the ops
teams, and we're done.

00:06:45.620 --> 00:06:48.220
It's not my problem, they
take care of it-- woo!

00:06:48.220 --> 00:06:51.719
In my company in my old days,
we were pretty friendly.

00:06:51.719 --> 00:06:52.510
We knew each other.

00:06:52.510 --> 00:06:53.343
We liked each other.

00:06:53.343 --> 00:06:56.367
So we would refer to them
as those poor bastards who

00:06:56.367 --> 00:06:58.200
had to handle the crap
we gave them, and had

00:06:58.200 --> 00:07:00.450
a lot of out-of-memory
errors, and stuff like that.

00:07:00.450 --> 00:07:02.550
And they would call
us those idiots

00:07:02.550 --> 00:07:04.280
who keep sending us this stuff.

00:07:04.280 --> 00:07:06.030
And we had a positive
relationship, right?

00:07:06.030 --> 00:07:10.920
So we have silos
and inefficiencies.

00:07:10.920 --> 00:07:13.590
It really boils down
to cost and slowness.

00:07:13.590 --> 00:07:15.160
We all know what
I'm talking about.

00:07:15.160 --> 00:07:17.820
Today, we've moved
ahead many years.

00:07:17.820 --> 00:07:19.140
Nowadays, it's your problem.

00:07:19.140 --> 00:07:22.650
It's your team's problem.

00:07:22.650 --> 00:07:25.890
Remember, DevOps is
not a title, or it

00:07:25.890 --> 00:07:28.180
shouldn't be just a title
of someone on your team.

00:07:28.180 --> 00:07:29.700
It's a whole team culture.

00:07:29.700 --> 00:07:32.280
So the QA is now the problem
of every person on the team.

00:07:32.280 --> 00:07:34.317
You're responsible
for the code you ship,

00:07:34.317 --> 00:07:35.400
that the deployment works.

00:07:35.400 --> 00:07:37.160
If you're causing
out-of-memory errors,

00:07:37.160 --> 00:07:38.580
someone's going to tell
your team about it,

00:07:38.580 --> 00:07:39.455
and then they fix it.

00:07:39.455 --> 00:07:40.790
This is the way it works.

00:07:40.790 --> 00:07:43.200
But you just want to
focus on that application.

00:07:43.200 --> 00:07:44.970
So you're going
to need a tooling

00:07:44.970 --> 00:07:51.009
to make this not your problem,
or at least a solved problem.

00:07:51.009 --> 00:07:53.550
What would this look like in an
ideal world for a development

00:07:53.550 --> 00:07:57.030
team and an ops team, a company
trying to run all of this

00:07:57.030 --> 00:07:57.530
together?

00:07:57.530 --> 00:07:59.279
You want every one of
your developer teams

00:07:59.279 --> 00:08:01.380
to write what they want
in a way that they can--

00:08:01.380 --> 00:08:04.364
in the language they want and
deploy it in the same way.

00:08:04.364 --> 00:08:05.390
Oh-- there will be more.

00:08:05.390 --> 00:08:06.660
Don't worry.

00:08:06.660 --> 00:08:08.446
You want that app
to just deploy.

00:08:08.446 --> 00:08:10.320
You just want to say,
here's the application,

00:08:10.320 --> 00:08:11.194
the structure I want.

00:08:11.194 --> 00:08:13.980
Make it so, and it's going
to live in this environment.

00:08:13.980 --> 00:08:15.964
I don't want to have
to worry about it.

00:08:15.964 --> 00:08:18.630
And hopefully, you don't have to
deal with the physical machines

00:08:18.630 --> 00:08:19.449
themselves, right?

00:08:19.449 --> 00:08:21.740
It's just you need a place
for this application to run.

00:08:21.740 --> 00:08:23.820
This environment is
going to give them

00:08:23.820 --> 00:08:27.810
the space to run that you
don't have to stress out about.

00:08:27.810 --> 00:08:29.579
So what would such
a solution look

00:08:29.579 --> 00:08:30.870
like in the modern cloud world?

00:08:30.870 --> 00:08:33.270
Well, we'd have
container packaging

00:08:33.270 --> 00:08:36.120
for predictable
deployment, dynamic

00:08:36.120 --> 00:08:39.510
scheduling to make sure that
our application found a place

00:08:39.510 --> 00:08:43.440
to run, and recomputing
that when necessary.

00:08:43.440 --> 00:08:45.600
And it's got to be
microservices oriented, right?

00:08:45.600 --> 00:08:46.920
It's got to be
something that supports

00:08:46.920 --> 00:08:49.260
this type of thing-- reusability
of code, fast feature

00:08:49.260 --> 00:08:50.776
release, loose coupling.

00:08:50.776 --> 00:08:51.900
So let's start the journey.

00:08:51.900 --> 00:08:53.380
And wow, we're
really short on time.

00:08:53.380 --> 00:08:55.921
Unless this clock's a little--
started before I got on stage.

00:08:58.650 --> 00:09:00.630
We tried this running on VMs.

00:09:00.630 --> 00:09:02.160
It didn't quite deliver.

00:09:02.160 --> 00:09:05.730
We never quite got the
efficiency that we wanted.

00:09:05.730 --> 00:09:08.100
We never quite got the
automation we wanted.

00:09:08.100 --> 00:09:11.790
It works, but it requires
a lot of extra work.

00:09:11.790 --> 00:09:15.180
Just skipping ahead from that,
the modern developer world,

00:09:15.180 --> 00:09:17.190
and the DevOps,
and the ops teams

00:09:17.190 --> 00:09:18.810
are falling in love
with containers.

00:09:18.810 --> 00:09:21.430
We abstract away the operating
system of the containers.

00:09:21.430 --> 00:09:24.597
Your application only needs
to think about its interfaces

00:09:24.597 --> 00:09:26.430
with other applications
in your environment.

00:09:26.430 --> 00:09:27.846
It doesn't need
to think about all

00:09:27.846 --> 00:09:29.230
these load-balancing things.

00:09:29.230 --> 00:09:31.230
They give our development
teams a consistent way

00:09:31.230 --> 00:09:33.104
to package our applications
and deliver them,

00:09:33.104 --> 00:09:34.950
and dependencies so my
laptop doesn't end up

00:09:34.950 --> 00:09:36.780
in the data center again.

00:09:36.780 --> 00:09:39.090
It's a great solution to
many of these problems.

00:09:39.090 --> 00:09:43.470
And if we only had one machine
that never failed and never ran

00:09:43.470 --> 00:09:45.870
out of resources, we'd
put them all on it.

00:09:45.870 --> 00:09:47.857
And life would be
good, and we'd be done.

00:09:47.857 --> 00:09:49.440
But that's not the
real world, either.

00:09:49.440 --> 00:09:52.710
So we've figured out dockers
are great, containers are great.

00:09:52.710 --> 00:09:55.234
But now we have a new
set of challenges.

00:09:55.234 --> 00:09:57.150
Computers-- everything
you solve-- oh, there's

00:09:57.150 --> 00:09:59.440
a new challenge behind it.

00:09:59.440 --> 00:10:01.260
So once we fall in
love with containers

00:10:01.260 --> 00:10:04.530
we try to build a system with
50 services, microservices,

00:10:04.530 --> 00:10:07.740
built out of 500 running
containers across 30 machines.

00:10:07.740 --> 00:10:10.680
And we discover that there's
a new layer of complexity

00:10:10.680 --> 00:10:12.300
how we do these things.

00:10:12.300 --> 00:10:14.970
Luckily, Google has some
experience with this.

00:10:14.970 --> 00:10:18.770
We've been running containers in
our data centers for 12 years.

00:10:18.770 --> 00:10:20.910
So we start about 2
billion containers a week--

00:10:20.910 --> 00:10:24.360
Gmail, Search, Maps.

00:10:24.360 --> 00:10:27.140
Everything you touch at Google,
you're touching a container.

00:10:27.140 --> 00:10:29.430
It's running on a container.

00:10:29.430 --> 00:10:32.640
The point of that is
we have some experience

00:10:32.640 --> 00:10:35.820
at understanding how
to structure containers

00:10:35.820 --> 00:10:38.430
in production and
run them efficiently.

00:10:38.430 --> 00:10:41.370
So about 2 and 1/2
or 3 years ago,

00:10:41.370 --> 00:10:43.380
we launched this really
cool open source project

00:10:43.380 --> 00:10:44.730
called Kubernetes.

00:10:44.730 --> 00:10:46.350
We took the lessons
we'd learned,

00:10:46.350 --> 00:10:48.900
we packaged them up into
an open-source community,

00:10:48.900 --> 00:10:50.550
we gave it to the
Linux Foundation,

00:10:50.550 --> 00:10:53.970
the Cloud Native
Computing Foundation.

00:10:53.970 --> 00:10:56.820
Recruited community members
like crazy, tons of people

00:10:56.820 --> 00:10:58.200
doing stuff on it.

00:10:58.200 --> 00:11:01.890
And basically, we
built Kubernetes

00:11:01.890 --> 00:11:03.390
to solve this problem.

00:11:03.390 --> 00:11:05.726
We wanted to expose the
way we work to the world.

00:11:05.726 --> 00:11:07.350
We wanted people to
be able to do this.

00:11:11.210 --> 00:11:14.010
It's a great orchestrator for
your containers in production.

00:11:14.010 --> 00:11:15.514
You can run it anywhere.

00:11:15.514 --> 00:11:17.430
But when you run it at
Google, a lot of things

00:11:17.430 --> 00:11:18.900
are automatically
handled for you.

00:11:18.900 --> 00:11:20.867
Like the master, which
is your control point--

00:11:20.867 --> 00:11:22.950
I don't have any architecture
diagrams about that.

00:11:26.702 --> 00:11:28.410
It's managed by Google,
so you don't have

00:11:28.410 --> 00:11:30.657
to worry about upgrades, nodes.

00:11:30.657 --> 00:11:33.240
You tell the master the way you
want your application to look.

00:11:33.240 --> 00:11:35.130
It gives you the
nodes to run on.

00:11:35.130 --> 00:11:37.800
If you need more or less,
it takes care of that.

00:11:37.800 --> 00:11:40.860
It decides where to
stick those applications.

00:11:40.860 --> 00:11:43.770
It routes traffic to them so
outside users can find them,

00:11:43.770 --> 00:11:45.750
and your internal
services can find them.

00:11:45.750 --> 00:11:47.880
And it's responsible for
setting up and keeping it

00:11:47.880 --> 00:11:50.400
that way as life goes
on, and things break,

00:11:50.400 --> 00:11:51.780
and things change.

00:11:51.780 --> 00:11:54.620
So I'm going to go
into technical details

00:11:54.620 --> 00:11:56.520
in the second part.

00:11:56.520 --> 00:11:59.010
But first, I'm going to
hand it over to Jean Mertz,

00:11:59.010 --> 00:12:01.559
the CTO of Blendle.

00:12:01.559 --> 00:12:03.600
His company has been on
this journey for a while,

00:12:03.600 --> 00:12:05.225
and he's going to
share his information

00:12:05.225 --> 00:12:07.770
about that experience of
moving into microservices,

00:12:07.770 --> 00:12:11.400
from monolith to microservices,
and how Kubernetes helped them

00:12:11.400 --> 00:12:12.220
with that.

00:12:12.220 --> 00:12:14.324
So Jean, come on up!

00:12:14.324 --> 00:12:15.240
Thanks for joining me.

00:12:15.240 --> 00:12:15.500
JEAN MERTZ: Thanks, man.

00:12:15.500 --> 00:12:16.800
MATT FEIGAL: I hand over.

00:12:16.800 --> 00:12:18.570
JEAN MERTZ: Thank you.

00:12:18.570 --> 00:12:20.020
Morning, everyone.

00:12:20.020 --> 00:12:23.400
So I'm going to try to be as
enthusiastic as Matt was just

00:12:23.400 --> 00:12:23.950
now.

00:12:23.950 --> 00:12:27.810
But those are some tough shoes
to fill, so bear with me.

00:12:27.810 --> 00:12:30.510
So just a quick question first.

00:12:30.510 --> 00:12:33.840
Who have you have heard
of Blendle before?

00:12:33.840 --> 00:12:35.197
Great, quite some hands.

00:12:35.197 --> 00:12:37.030
For those of you who
didn't raise your hand,

00:12:37.030 --> 00:12:39.870
let me just quickly
introduce you to our product.

00:12:39.870 --> 00:12:41.910
So Blendle is an
online kiosk that

00:12:41.910 --> 00:12:44.640
helps you find the journalism
and background stories that

00:12:44.640 --> 00:12:46.020
are interesting to you.

00:12:46.020 --> 00:12:48.570
We present you the best stories
you should read every morning

00:12:48.570 --> 00:12:51.450
and deliver them to you via
email, via the native apps

00:12:51.450 --> 00:12:52.950
that we have, et cetera.

00:12:52.950 --> 00:12:54.900
There are no pay walls, no ads.

00:12:54.900 --> 00:12:58.320
It is you and great journalism.

00:12:58.320 --> 00:13:00.840
You either pay with a
few cents per article,

00:13:00.840 --> 00:13:03.540
or last January we launched
Blendle Premium, in which you

00:13:03.540 --> 00:13:05.970
pay a fixed fee
every month, and we

00:13:05.970 --> 00:13:07.830
send you a selection
of articles that

00:13:07.830 --> 00:13:09.790
are most interesting to you.

00:13:09.790 --> 00:13:11.730
We also have a staff of
editors who make sure

00:13:11.730 --> 00:13:15.600
that we try to burst your
filter bubble everyday again.

00:13:18.580 --> 00:13:21.530
Once you click on an
article, you can read it.

00:13:21.530 --> 00:13:23.710
And we are very proud
of the UI and UX

00:13:23.710 --> 00:13:25.664
that we put a lot of time in.

00:13:25.664 --> 00:13:27.830
So this is the interface
that you get when you read.

00:13:27.830 --> 00:13:29.871
And you can see it's all
about you and the story.

00:13:29.871 --> 00:13:34.400
It's presented in the way that
the author meant it to be read.

00:13:34.400 --> 00:13:37.257
So there are no ads,
no annoying pop-ups.

00:13:37.257 --> 00:13:39.590
And we have a huge selection
of newspapers and magazines

00:13:39.590 --> 00:13:40.770
you can pick from.

00:13:40.770 --> 00:13:43.100
You see "Bloomberg Business
Week," "Time" magazine.

00:13:43.100 --> 00:13:46.400
We also have "New York Times,"
"Economist," "Mother Jones,"

00:13:46.400 --> 00:13:49.470
"Wall Street Journal," "The New
Yorker," et cetera, et cetera.

00:13:49.470 --> 00:13:51.850
So there's always something
interesting to you.

00:13:51.850 --> 00:13:53.915
If you want to check out
more, go to Blendle.com

00:13:53.915 --> 00:13:56.660
or download the app
in the App Store.

00:13:56.660 --> 00:13:58.340
But let's talk about
why you're here.

00:13:58.340 --> 00:14:01.400
Let's talk about
our technical stack.

00:14:01.400 --> 00:14:05.210
So we have currently
got 32 employees--

00:14:05.210 --> 00:14:08.950
actually, 32 engineers
working on our product.

00:14:08.950 --> 00:14:10.700
We are a polyglot
development environment,

00:14:10.700 --> 00:14:13.260
which means we use Ruby
for back-end stuff,

00:14:13.260 --> 00:14:15.650
but we also use Python
for data scientists.

00:14:15.650 --> 00:14:18.080
We use GoLine for where
we need performance,

00:14:18.080 --> 00:14:19.705
and a lot of other languages.

00:14:19.705 --> 00:14:21.080
And this is
something which we'll

00:14:21.080 --> 00:14:24.290
get into where
containers really help.

00:14:24.290 --> 00:14:26.000
We've also got native
apps and a website

00:14:26.000 --> 00:14:28.340
which is built with React.

00:14:28.340 --> 00:14:32.020
We started out with a monolith,
but which startup doesn't?

00:14:32.020 --> 00:14:33.770
And we'll also get
into that, because it's

00:14:33.770 --> 00:14:37.474
important to realize that
starting with a monolith

00:14:37.474 --> 00:14:39.140
is key to understanding
how you're going

00:14:39.140 --> 00:14:41.720
to implement microservices.

00:14:41.720 --> 00:14:44.690
But in the past year, we've been
slowly but surely splitting off

00:14:44.690 --> 00:14:47.737
microservices from
that monolith.

00:14:47.737 --> 00:14:49.070
So let me give you some context.

00:14:49.070 --> 00:14:51.080
There are some numbers
here about our production

00:14:51.080 --> 00:14:52.520
applications.

00:14:52.520 --> 00:14:53.990
The most interesting ones--

00:14:53.990 --> 00:14:56.810
we are generating more than 50
million personalized bundles

00:14:56.810 --> 00:14:58.280
every month.

00:14:58.280 --> 00:15:00.077
And we're sending
more than 400,000

00:15:00.077 --> 00:15:01.910
personalized newsletters
in under 10 minutes

00:15:01.910 --> 00:15:04.160
to all of our customers.

00:15:04.160 --> 00:15:06.650
And this is really key when
you look at microservices,

00:15:06.650 --> 00:15:10.370
because depending on
the needs that you have

00:15:10.370 --> 00:15:13.020
for your small part
of your application,

00:15:13.020 --> 00:15:15.800
you might decide to build your
application in a specific way

00:15:15.800 --> 00:15:17.760
or with a specific language.

00:15:17.760 --> 00:15:19.880
If you need simplicity,
you use a language

00:15:19.880 --> 00:15:22.280
that's easy to work with
and easy to understand.

00:15:22.280 --> 00:15:25.940
If you need performance, you
use C++ or Go, or whatever.

00:15:25.940 --> 00:15:28.790
So it's key to understand
your application

00:15:28.790 --> 00:15:31.850
and when to use what.

00:15:31.850 --> 00:15:35.060
So our journey brought us from
the monolith on bare metal

00:15:35.060 --> 00:15:37.640
to microservices on a
container-based cloud

00:15:37.640 --> 00:15:39.230
infrastructure.

00:15:39.230 --> 00:15:42.002
And there's more to come in the
coming years, I'm sure of that.

00:15:42.002 --> 00:15:43.460
There is a big
community around it,

00:15:43.460 --> 00:15:46.920
as we saw in the Keynote area.

00:15:46.920 --> 00:15:50.000
So let's talk
about the monolith.

00:15:50.000 --> 00:15:54.350
It used to be, and still is, a
Ruby-based API with JavaScript

00:15:54.350 --> 00:15:57.944
front-end and native apps
communicating with the API.

00:15:57.944 --> 00:15:59.360
And surrounding
that API were lots

00:15:59.360 --> 00:16:03.270
of different object stores,
databases, caches, proxies, et

00:16:03.270 --> 00:16:04.460
cetera.

00:16:04.460 --> 00:16:08.300
But it all revolves
around that single API,

00:16:08.300 --> 00:16:11.720
and thus we're dependent
on that API always working.

00:16:11.720 --> 00:16:16.250
We did manual deployments
using Capistrano.

00:16:16.250 --> 00:16:19.430
We ran our application
on three machines.

00:16:19.430 --> 00:16:21.925
They were running here in
a data center in Amsterdam.

00:16:21.925 --> 00:16:23.300
And we had an
operations engineer

00:16:23.300 --> 00:16:26.175
responsible for the production
environment of our product.

00:16:26.175 --> 00:16:27.800
So there are obviously
some pain points

00:16:27.800 --> 00:16:31.070
here that we wanted to solve.

00:16:31.070 --> 00:16:32.600
For example, these three.

00:16:32.600 --> 00:16:34.670
Manual deployments,
we can all agree--

00:16:34.670 --> 00:16:37.380
takes a lot of time, and it
includes a higher potential

00:16:37.380 --> 00:16:38.250
of human error.

00:16:38.250 --> 00:16:40.254
So you need to get
away with that.

00:16:40.254 --> 00:16:42.170
Running a single application
on a few machines

00:16:42.170 --> 00:16:45.170
also means that you're not
using the full utilization

00:16:45.170 --> 00:16:47.780
of those machines, thus
increasing the cost of running

00:16:47.780 --> 00:16:49.850
your application.

00:16:49.850 --> 00:16:51.350
And having development
of production

00:16:51.350 --> 00:16:54.170
be managed by different
teams automatically means

00:16:54.170 --> 00:16:56.030
that there is less
accountability.

00:16:56.030 --> 00:16:58.830
Different people are
responsible for different parts,

00:16:58.830 --> 00:17:02.199
different lifecycle stages
of your application.

00:17:02.199 --> 00:17:03.740
And that can complicate
things a lot.

00:17:06.829 --> 00:17:09.560
But the other two could also
be scratched off this list.

00:17:09.560 --> 00:17:13.040
Because, like I said before,
using a single API which

00:17:13.040 --> 00:17:16.396
has to be performant, and
easy, et cetera, is difficult.

00:17:16.396 --> 00:17:18.770
There is not one true language
which can handle this all,

00:17:18.770 --> 00:17:22.250
or one true environment
which can handle this all.

00:17:22.250 --> 00:17:26.480
So we moved to microservices.

00:17:26.480 --> 00:17:28.369
We've been doing this
for some time now.

00:17:28.369 --> 00:17:34.160
And actually, what we noticed
is that the technology

00:17:34.160 --> 00:17:39.300
problems aren't the problems
that you should worry about.

00:17:39.300 --> 00:17:41.640
You have a lot of smart
engineers at your company.

00:17:41.640 --> 00:17:42.925
They know how to solve this.

00:17:42.925 --> 00:17:44.300
But besides that,
there are a lot

00:17:44.300 --> 00:17:46.420
of smart people working
at Google, at Red hat,

00:17:46.420 --> 00:17:47.900
at other companies.

00:17:47.900 --> 00:17:49.335
They put tons of hours--

00:17:49.335 --> 00:17:51.710
again, if you saw early in
the Keynote, put tons of hours

00:17:51.710 --> 00:17:54.830
in working on these
projects like Kubernetes

00:17:54.830 --> 00:17:57.200
to help you solve
those problems.

00:17:57.200 --> 00:18:00.560
In fact, it's about conceptual
problems and processes

00:18:00.560 --> 00:18:03.744
that intertwine with your
stack, your application.

00:18:03.744 --> 00:18:05.660
That's something outside
people can't work on,

00:18:05.660 --> 00:18:09.320
because it's your team
who has to work on that.

00:18:09.320 --> 00:18:11.510
And that's what made
this interesting.

00:18:11.510 --> 00:18:13.910
For example, the
biggest challenge we saw

00:18:13.910 --> 00:18:16.550
is that defining what
part of your monolith

00:18:16.550 --> 00:18:19.650
you're going to split off
into microservices is key.

00:18:19.650 --> 00:18:22.040
You don't just take
a part like, this

00:18:22.040 --> 00:18:24.680
looks like a good
block of content

00:18:24.680 --> 00:18:27.110
that we can take and
put into a microservice.

00:18:27.110 --> 00:18:30.170
You have to think about
the domain boundaries.

00:18:30.170 --> 00:18:32.180
If you don't do
that, you'll actually

00:18:32.180 --> 00:18:34.750
end up right back where
you started, but worse.

00:18:34.750 --> 00:18:37.340
You'll have a monolith
split between microservices

00:18:37.340 --> 00:18:39.920
which has to communicate
over the networking layer

00:18:39.920 --> 00:18:43.700
to call different functions
in different microservices.

00:18:43.700 --> 00:18:46.610
And we all know adding a
network layer increases the risk

00:18:46.610 --> 00:18:49.220
of hard-to-debug errors.

00:18:49.220 --> 00:18:50.816
So always go the
extra mile, not only

00:18:50.816 --> 00:18:53.065
to make sure that you
understand the different domains

00:18:53.065 --> 00:18:54.902
of your product,
but also to prevent

00:18:54.902 --> 00:18:56.360
too much chatter
and too much tight

00:18:56.360 --> 00:18:58.250
coupling between your services.

00:18:58.250 --> 00:19:01.400
And this is key to having
a successful microservice

00:19:01.400 --> 00:19:02.990
oriented architecture.

00:19:02.990 --> 00:19:05.500
If you don't, like I said,
you will probably end up back

00:19:05.500 --> 00:19:08.374
where you were in the beginning.

00:19:08.374 --> 00:19:10.760
And Martin Fowler-- he was
a famous software architect

00:19:10.760 --> 00:19:11.367
and author.

00:19:11.367 --> 00:19:12.950
He works at ThoughtWorks
and he's also

00:19:12.950 --> 00:19:15.530
one of the original authors
of the "Manifesto for Agile

00:19:15.530 --> 00:19:17.330
Software Development."

00:19:17.330 --> 00:19:18.330
I think he said it best.

00:19:18.330 --> 00:19:19.790
He said, "Almost
all the cases where

00:19:19.790 --> 00:19:21.110
I've heard of a
system that was built

00:19:21.110 --> 00:19:22.820
as a microservice
system from scratch,

00:19:22.820 --> 00:19:25.580
it has ended up in
serious trouble."

00:19:25.580 --> 00:19:27.949
You need to have production
experience with your domains

00:19:27.949 --> 00:19:30.490
in order to understand where it
makes sense to split them off

00:19:30.490 --> 00:19:32.450
and where it doesn't.

00:19:32.450 --> 00:19:34.322
Or taken another way,
he said, "Almost all

00:19:34.322 --> 00:19:35.780
the successful
microservice stories

00:19:35.780 --> 00:19:37.670
have started with a
monolith that got too big

00:19:37.670 --> 00:19:39.440
and was broken up."

00:19:39.440 --> 00:19:42.690
So this is very important.

00:19:42.690 --> 00:19:46.040
So how does Kubernetes
fit into this picture?

00:19:46.040 --> 00:19:48.767
Like I said before,
once you figure out

00:19:48.767 --> 00:19:50.600
how you want to split
up your microservices,

00:19:50.600 --> 00:19:53.440
the technology isn't
really the problem.

00:19:53.440 --> 00:19:55.490
Kubernetes will help
you with that part.

00:19:55.490 --> 00:19:57.180
Of course, it takes time.

00:19:57.180 --> 00:19:59.810
Of course, there are challenges
you have to overcome.

00:19:59.810 --> 00:20:03.376
But as far as we've seen,
you can overcome them,

00:20:03.376 --> 00:20:05.000
and there is a big
community around you

00:20:05.000 --> 00:20:07.100
to help you with that.

00:20:07.100 --> 00:20:09.320
I think Kubernetes
turned out to be

00:20:09.320 --> 00:20:12.140
much more than a simple
container schedule for us.

00:20:12.140 --> 00:20:14.390
It was a way to
empower our employees

00:20:14.390 --> 00:20:17.330
to experiment and self-manage.

00:20:17.330 --> 00:20:22.100
We actually saw an increase in
teams and individuals deploying

00:20:22.100 --> 00:20:24.350
simple applications
that they built,

00:20:24.350 --> 00:20:27.327
either at the end of the day,
or during a day where there

00:20:27.327 --> 00:20:28.910
was nothing else
they were working on,

00:20:28.910 --> 00:20:33.050
or they a built some cool
project on the weekends.

00:20:33.050 --> 00:20:37.400
These things can then easily be
deployed to your entire company

00:20:37.400 --> 00:20:39.500
and then be used by people.

00:20:39.500 --> 00:20:42.140
And that fosters innovation
and increasing speed

00:20:42.140 --> 00:20:45.290
of your deployment cycles.

00:20:45.290 --> 00:20:47.210
So these are some numbers
about our Kubernetes

00:20:47.210 --> 00:20:47.840
cluster [INAUDIBLE].

00:20:47.840 --> 00:20:50.131
We host our Kubernetes cluster
on GCE, Google Container

00:20:50.131 --> 00:20:51.500
Engine.

00:20:51.500 --> 00:20:54.290
We have eight nodes, of which
six are preemptable VMs.

00:20:54.290 --> 00:20:58.330
And on those are
running 575 pods.

00:20:58.330 --> 00:21:00.740
134 services.

00:21:00.740 --> 00:21:02.930
And what's interesting about
this is that because we

00:21:02.930 --> 00:21:05.087
use preemptable VMs--

00:21:05.087 --> 00:21:06.920
there will be a talk
about this later today,

00:21:06.920 --> 00:21:08.570
I think, about what
those are, in case

00:21:08.570 --> 00:21:09.778
you don't know what they are.

00:21:09.778 --> 00:21:14.690
But to quickly explain, a
preemptable VM allows you to--

00:21:14.690 --> 00:21:17.480
it gives you a machine
which can, at any time,

00:21:17.480 --> 00:21:20.750
be taken back by Google because
they need the resources.

00:21:20.750 --> 00:21:24.244
But you get a steep
discount on these machines.

00:21:24.244 --> 00:21:25.910
And because we're
running on Kubernetes,

00:21:25.910 --> 00:21:29.850
and we are scaling up, and
we have multiple replicates

00:21:29.850 --> 00:21:33.290
per service running, it doesn't
matter if a machine drops out.

00:21:33.290 --> 00:21:35.090
Because the application
will stay up.

00:21:35.090 --> 00:21:37.310
And what it does bring
us is a reduced cost.

00:21:37.310 --> 00:21:41.330
And it brings us automated chaos
monkey-like infrastructure,

00:21:41.330 --> 00:21:42.924
where machines will
fill all the time,

00:21:42.924 --> 00:21:44.840
and then our engineers
need to be wary of that

00:21:44.840 --> 00:21:46.850
and need to design
their applications to be

00:21:46.850 --> 00:21:50.460
highly available.

00:21:50.460 --> 00:21:53.261
So what does Kubernetes
mean for us in practice?

00:21:53.261 --> 00:21:56.180
All the services are highly
available, like I said.

00:21:56.180 --> 00:21:59.105
Even the most simple ones we
replicate at least three times,

00:21:59.105 --> 00:22:00.480
just because we
want to make sure

00:22:00.480 --> 00:22:02.063
that it's always
running, even if it's

00:22:02.063 --> 00:22:03.712
an internal application.

00:22:03.712 --> 00:22:06.170
And combined with microservices
with clearly defined domain

00:22:06.170 --> 00:22:08.770
boundaries, like I
told about earlier,

00:22:08.770 --> 00:22:10.550
it means we haven't
had a complete failure

00:22:10.550 --> 00:22:13.850
of our application within at
least the last year or maybe

00:22:13.850 --> 00:22:15.230
more.

00:22:15.230 --> 00:22:17.160
Things do still
break, of course.

00:22:17.160 --> 00:22:18.110
We're only human.

00:22:18.110 --> 00:22:20.090
But the impact of
something breaking

00:22:20.090 --> 00:22:22.790
isn't as big as Blendle is down.

00:22:22.790 --> 00:22:25.130
It might be as big as
search isn't working.

00:22:25.130 --> 00:22:27.110
Or the email is delayed,
and is not on time

00:22:27.110 --> 00:22:29.970
to our customers, which is
bad, and we to fix that.

00:22:29.970 --> 00:22:33.320
But people can still
use our product.

00:22:33.320 --> 00:22:36.380
And on top of that, we went from
a couple of manual deployments

00:22:36.380 --> 00:22:40.910
every week to sometimes
50 releases per day.

00:22:40.910 --> 00:22:42.890
All our applications
are automatically

00:22:42.890 --> 00:22:46.050
tested and
automatically deployed.

00:22:46.050 --> 00:22:49.880
So let's take a quick look
at some real-world use cases.

00:22:49.880 --> 00:22:51.470
For example, Blendle
Button, which

00:22:51.470 --> 00:22:54.800
is a product that we
built where, in this case,

00:22:54.800 --> 00:22:58.340
the folks can put
it on their website,

00:22:58.340 --> 00:23:02.460
and people can buy the article
for a few cents with the wallet

00:23:02.460 --> 00:23:04.120
they have at Blendle.

00:23:04.120 --> 00:23:06.260
And we ship that in
a couple of months,

00:23:06.260 --> 00:23:08.480
and we actually-- this
was our first microservice

00:23:08.480 --> 00:23:11.240
that we built on
top of Kubernetes.

00:23:11.240 --> 00:23:13.370
I think Kubernetes was
still version 0.9 back then,

00:23:13.370 --> 00:23:15.470
so I would say don't
try this at home,

00:23:15.470 --> 00:23:18.440
but eventually, things work out.

00:23:18.440 --> 00:23:20.540
Also, Goals is an
interesting use case,

00:23:20.540 --> 00:23:24.740
because a product manager, lead
product manager at our company,

00:23:24.740 --> 00:23:28.070
wanted a tool that could
help us focus on our OKRs

00:23:28.070 --> 00:23:31.160
and on personal goal-setting
that all engineers at Blendle

00:23:31.160 --> 00:23:31.830
have.

00:23:31.830 --> 00:23:34.880
He built a quick tool, used
Ruby on Rails, real simple.

00:23:34.880 --> 00:23:36.830
Bootstrap for the front-end.

00:23:36.830 --> 00:23:38.900
And he deployed
it on our cluster,

00:23:38.900 --> 00:23:41.380
and everyone is using it
at our company right now.

00:23:41.380 --> 00:23:43.460
So it's really awesome.

00:23:43.460 --> 00:23:47.420
Something like this, where
two engineers at Blendle

00:23:47.420 --> 00:23:49.670
build a spreadsheet which
automatically queries

00:23:49.670 --> 00:23:54.252
the Kubernetes API in which
every team who deploys

00:23:54.252 --> 00:23:56.585
an application, Kubernetes
adds a little bit of metadata

00:23:56.585 --> 00:23:59.720
to the deployment where
people can find support

00:23:59.720 --> 00:24:00.660
for this application.

00:24:00.660 --> 00:24:03.860
So adding a name, multiple
names, adding in git-repo,

00:24:03.860 --> 00:24:05.720
adding in email address,
stuff like that.

00:24:05.720 --> 00:24:08.840
And then it's automatically
consolidated into a spreadsheet

00:24:08.840 --> 00:24:11.520
for people to find out, for
example, the support team,

00:24:11.520 --> 00:24:13.680
like who can I ask
about this problem?

00:24:13.680 --> 00:24:15.840
So these are some examples.

00:24:15.840 --> 00:24:17.780
Continuous deployment
is another big one.

00:24:17.780 --> 00:24:20.960
We are actually running our own
continuous deployment pipeline

00:24:20.960 --> 00:24:22.580
on top of Kubernetes.

00:24:22.580 --> 00:24:26.210
So we deploy to Kubernetes
from a continuous deployment

00:24:26.210 --> 00:24:28.010
pipeline which is
run on Kubernetes,

00:24:28.010 --> 00:24:31.172
and it auto-scales for
whatever requirements

00:24:31.172 --> 00:24:32.130
we have at that moment.

00:24:32.130 --> 00:24:34.162
So if a lot of teams are
deploying at that time,

00:24:34.162 --> 00:24:35.120
it just auto-scales it.

00:24:35.120 --> 00:24:37.130
It adds more nodes,
it adds more pods,

00:24:37.130 --> 00:24:40.490
and it's able to
deploy in a couple

00:24:40.490 --> 00:24:45.110
seconds, or for bigger projects,
in a couple of minutes.

00:24:45.110 --> 00:24:48.800
To recap, I think
starting with a monolith,

00:24:48.800 --> 00:24:50.660
or at least having a
clear understanding

00:24:50.660 --> 00:24:54.170
on why you need
microservices, is key.

00:24:54.170 --> 00:24:55.940
If you use Kubernetes,
and in our example,

00:24:55.940 --> 00:24:58.765
Google Container Engine, the
problem becomes less technical.

00:24:58.765 --> 00:25:00.890
You need to focus on the
operational and conceptual

00:25:00.890 --> 00:25:03.227
parts of these problems.

00:25:03.227 --> 00:25:04.310
Teams become self-managed.

00:25:04.310 --> 00:25:06.320
They can release better,
they can release more.

00:25:06.320 --> 00:25:09.350
You empower your teams.

00:25:09.350 --> 00:25:12.020
And as for us, you can
release a new service

00:25:12.020 --> 00:25:13.160
in less than an hour.

00:25:13.160 --> 00:25:14.962
If someone has an ID,
they built something,

00:25:14.962 --> 00:25:16.670
we have a continuous
deployment pipeline.

00:25:16.670 --> 00:25:19.610
They can ship it.

00:25:19.610 --> 00:25:21.200
Thanks everyone for
your time, and I'd

00:25:21.200 --> 00:25:22.408
like to give it back to Matt.

00:25:22.408 --> 00:25:24.305
[APPLAUSE]

00:25:26.120 --> 00:25:29.030
MATT FEIGAL: Thank
you very much, sir.

00:25:29.030 --> 00:25:31.260
Hey, everyone!

00:25:31.260 --> 00:25:33.770
Thanks for letting
me back on stage.

00:25:33.770 --> 00:25:36.230
I do need to keep moving
fast, but I'll also

00:25:36.230 --> 00:25:39.950
try to keep up a little
energy through this next part

00:25:39.950 --> 00:25:42.710
of the technical story.

00:25:42.710 --> 00:25:45.800
What is it about Kubernetes
that makes it such a great place

00:25:45.800 --> 00:25:49.700
to run many, many microservices
in a resilient way

00:25:49.700 --> 00:25:52.280
and empower your
teams like this?

00:25:52.280 --> 00:25:56.360
Kubernetes has many
powerful cases and features.

00:25:56.360 --> 00:25:58.880
They've been built over
the last 2 and 1/2 years

00:25:58.880 --> 00:26:02.240
by the open source community and
Google and other contributing

00:26:02.240 --> 00:26:04.160
partners working together.

00:26:04.160 --> 00:26:06.959
Companies like yours,
companies like Blendle.

00:26:06.959 --> 00:26:08.750
We launched it to the
open source community

00:26:08.750 --> 00:26:11.630
with the intention of
growing these use cases,

00:26:11.630 --> 00:26:13.940
because we know how
Google used these things.

00:26:13.940 --> 00:26:15.890
We needed to know how
you needed to use them.

00:26:15.890 --> 00:26:18.110
And the community has
built up these use cases.

00:26:18.110 --> 00:26:20.450
Underneath all of these
tools, all of these things

00:26:20.450 --> 00:26:23.990
that you wish you could do
with your microservices,

00:26:23.990 --> 00:26:27.464
I would say it boils down to
four key technical concepts.

00:26:27.464 --> 00:26:29.630
If you understand those
four key technical concepts,

00:26:29.630 --> 00:26:31.954
you understand everything
that's built on top of them

00:26:31.954 --> 00:26:33.620
in terms of how the
system works and why

00:26:33.620 --> 00:26:35.330
it's good for microservices.

00:26:35.330 --> 00:26:38.984
So I'm going to explain
pods, replica sets, services,

00:26:38.984 --> 00:26:41.150
and then labels, which is
how they all find and talk

00:26:41.150 --> 00:26:45.020
to each other, through the eye
of a microservice development

00:26:45.020 --> 00:26:46.930
team.

00:26:46.930 --> 00:26:49.900
Pods are the smallest
deployable unit.

00:26:49.900 --> 00:26:51.200
Why not a container?

00:26:51.200 --> 00:26:54.730
Well, we know from our
experience at Google--

00:26:54.730 --> 00:26:57.100
hard-won experience--
that sometimes you

00:26:57.100 --> 00:27:00.580
want to deploy multiple
containers together.

00:27:00.580 --> 00:27:02.140
They will live and die together.

00:27:02.140 --> 00:27:04.360
They share the same
cozy network space.

00:27:04.360 --> 00:27:07.330
They'll always deploy
together on the same node.

00:27:07.330 --> 00:27:09.800
You don't tell Kubernetes
to deploy a container.

00:27:09.800 --> 00:27:12.230
You tell it to deploy a pod.

00:27:12.230 --> 00:27:15.679
Now, often, a pod will
be just one container.

00:27:15.679 --> 00:27:17.470
Your front-end team
builds a Node.js thing.

00:27:17.470 --> 00:27:18.719
It doesn't need anything else.

00:27:18.719 --> 00:27:20.010
They put it in--

00:27:20.010 --> 00:27:22.990
they compile it in Docker,
ship it to the registry.

00:27:22.990 --> 00:27:26.130
You establish your template,
that this is my pod.

00:27:26.130 --> 00:27:28.120
That'll get deployed
on machines.

00:27:28.120 --> 00:27:30.772
Sometimes, it's not even
something you've built.

00:27:30.772 --> 00:27:32.230
You're using a
third-party software

00:27:32.230 --> 00:27:35.290
like Elasticsearch for
your product search.

00:27:35.290 --> 00:27:38.860
Kubernetes has tools in there,
like to attach a volume to it.

00:27:38.860 --> 00:27:42.940
And the template says, get
this Elasticsearch instance,

00:27:42.940 --> 00:27:47.190
get this volume that it's going
to need, put five copies of it

00:27:47.190 --> 00:27:49.730
in different nodes, you're
going to take care of it.

00:27:49.730 --> 00:27:53.120
It could also be
other languages.

00:27:53.120 --> 00:27:55.180
So say you're
streaming team says,

00:27:55.180 --> 00:27:57.130
for high-performance
stuff, we need Go.

00:27:57.130 --> 00:28:00.030
We also see something
introduced here now,

00:28:00.030 --> 00:28:04.600
a second container
in the same pod.

00:28:04.600 --> 00:28:06.970
We have this-- I would call
it a sidecar container.

00:28:06.970 --> 00:28:09.397
You could call it a helper
container, or maybe,

00:28:09.397 --> 00:28:11.230
in old-fashioned language,
you might call it

00:28:11.230 --> 00:28:13.115
cross-cutting concerns.

00:28:13.115 --> 00:28:14.740
Say you had something--
you said, well,

00:28:14.740 --> 00:28:19.100
we need the SSL offloading
to happen in a certain way.

00:28:19.100 --> 00:28:21.130
Let's have a team build
a container around that.

00:28:21.130 --> 00:28:22.630
We put it in the same pod.

00:28:22.630 --> 00:28:24.760
Our Go team doesn't
need to know it happens.

00:28:24.760 --> 00:28:27.824
Our Go team just gets
the message passed in.

00:28:27.824 --> 00:28:29.740
Without this, the Go
team would have to think,

00:28:29.740 --> 00:28:31.870
all right, now,
in my application,

00:28:31.870 --> 00:28:36.166
I have to build this SSL
piece into my application.

00:28:36.166 --> 00:28:37.040
Now, it doesn't care.

00:28:37.040 --> 00:28:39.950
It just gets the information
straight from the other pod.

00:28:39.950 --> 00:28:42.340
So these sidecar
containers really

00:28:42.340 --> 00:28:44.680
help you separate out these
cross-cutting concerns

00:28:44.680 --> 00:28:46.840
so you have less dependencies,
and your developers

00:28:46.840 --> 00:28:48.966
focus on just the
piece they want.

00:28:48.966 --> 00:28:50.590
And then it gets much
more complicated.

00:28:50.590 --> 00:28:53.830
You've got your Java team
with 10 years of experience,

00:28:53.830 --> 00:28:55.626
and they might also
have a logging agent

00:28:55.626 --> 00:28:57.250
that they use that
you want to separate

00:28:57.250 --> 00:28:59.780
from out of their code.

00:28:59.780 --> 00:29:03.870
Or even, for instance, you can
put .NET Core in a container

00:29:03.870 --> 00:29:06.427
now, which means you can
run it on Kubernetes.

00:29:06.427 --> 00:29:08.260
I've got a customer
sitting right there that

00:29:08.260 --> 00:29:09.550
does that in production.

00:29:09.550 --> 00:29:11.770
World Press Photos was on
stage a few minutes ago.

00:29:11.770 --> 00:29:13.400
They do this in production.

00:29:13.400 --> 00:29:17.110
So it's a new world that
we can do this stuff now.

00:29:17.110 --> 00:29:20.860
So on the microservices side,
let's take the big red button

00:29:20.860 --> 00:29:22.780
that Blendle wants to deploy.

00:29:22.780 --> 00:29:25.090
What does that look like
in Kubernetes world?

00:29:25.090 --> 00:29:27.730
The first thing we need to
know-- you understand pods, so

00:29:27.730 --> 00:29:29.980
the next thing to understand
is the replica set.

00:29:29.980 --> 00:29:32.500
This is essentially
just a control loop.

00:29:32.500 --> 00:29:35.160
You declare the state
you want it to be in.

00:29:35.160 --> 00:29:38.530
It's responsible for
pulling that pod you want,

00:29:38.530 --> 00:29:40.030
throwing on it
all of the nodes--

00:29:40.030 --> 00:29:41.920
doesn't matter which ones--

00:29:41.920 --> 00:29:43.310
making sure that
they're running.

00:29:43.310 --> 00:29:45.190
So it's going to do
these health check loops.

00:29:45.190 --> 00:29:47.800
If a pod dies, which is going
to happen-- things go wrong.

00:29:47.800 --> 00:29:51.920
You have an out-of-memory
error, a node crashes.

00:29:51.920 --> 00:29:54.055
Nobody gets alerted,
nobody gets woken up

00:29:54.055 --> 00:29:55.180
in the middle of the night.

00:29:55.180 --> 00:29:56.990
The replica set just
notices that and says,

00:29:56.990 --> 00:30:00.280
oh, let me start another one
somewhere else and fixes it.

00:30:00.280 --> 00:30:03.150
It's basically like the
"Star Trek" "make it so."

00:30:03.150 --> 00:30:06.040
So the replica set will
create the state you want

00:30:06.040 --> 00:30:08.620
and then keep the state
you want as things go wrong

00:30:08.620 --> 00:30:11.540
in your environment
and things change.

00:30:11.540 --> 00:30:13.850
The other side of that
microservice angle

00:30:13.850 --> 00:30:14.930
is the service.

00:30:14.930 --> 00:30:18.004
People need to reach it.

00:30:18.004 --> 00:30:19.670
Your outside audience
needs to reach it,

00:30:19.670 --> 00:30:22.180
or you're doing some
internal services.

00:30:22.180 --> 00:30:25.190
A service is a logical
grouping of pods

00:30:25.190 --> 00:30:28.880
that all have the same function
based on this label selector.

00:30:28.880 --> 00:30:31.310
So it's using this label,
looking for front-end.

00:30:31.310 --> 00:30:33.170
[GASPS] Oh, there's
a bug on my slide.

00:30:33.170 --> 00:30:36.310
It's looking for back-end, but
it's finding the front-end one.

00:30:36.310 --> 00:30:40.204
So that's a user bug, not the--

00:30:40.204 --> 00:30:42.620
Incoming requests are going
to be load-balanced across all

00:30:42.620 --> 00:30:44.390
of the pods that match.

00:30:44.390 --> 00:30:48.230
This means your service
gets a stable virtual IP,

00:30:48.230 --> 00:30:50.556
its own port, and
even its own DNS

00:30:50.556 --> 00:30:52.430
name, which you can use
for service discovery

00:30:52.430 --> 00:30:55.410
and routing inside.

00:30:55.410 --> 00:30:58.334
So all of that is handled
for you by Kubernetes.

00:30:58.334 --> 00:30:59.750
You put these two
things together,

00:30:59.750 --> 00:31:01.490
you have your microservice.

00:31:01.490 --> 00:31:03.560
The replica set and
the service combine

00:31:03.560 --> 00:31:05.350
to make your microservice.

00:31:05.350 --> 00:31:07.650
The replica set's taking
care of the resilience.

00:31:07.650 --> 00:31:10.800
The service is handling the
service discovery and routing.

00:31:10.800 --> 00:31:14.240
And now, bam, you've got people
hitting your big red button.

00:31:14.240 --> 00:31:17.052
People deploy it on their sites,
traffic's going to hit it.

00:31:17.052 --> 00:31:18.260
It's always going to be here.

00:31:18.260 --> 00:31:19.640
Kubernetes is going to
take care of that for you.

00:31:19.640 --> 00:31:21.560
Your application
developers didn't need

00:31:21.560 --> 00:31:23.100
to think about all this stuff.

00:31:23.100 --> 00:31:24.620
But that's not the end of it.

00:31:24.620 --> 00:31:26.210
We don't just create.

00:31:26.210 --> 00:31:28.730
We need to change.

00:31:28.730 --> 00:31:33.230
Big red button goes live,
it needs a lot of traffic.

00:31:33.230 --> 00:31:36.950
How do we scale it up as
we add more publishers?

00:31:36.950 --> 00:31:40.040
Well, this still leverages
the same core concepts.

00:31:40.040 --> 00:31:42.830
We just tell the replica set,
give me four instead of three,

00:31:42.830 --> 00:31:44.150
or 10 instead of three.

00:31:44.150 --> 00:31:46.729
And the replica set makes
it so-- bam, bam, bam.

00:31:46.729 --> 00:31:48.770
Now you've got 10 instances,
and the service just

00:31:48.770 --> 00:31:49.730
keeps finding them.

00:31:49.730 --> 00:31:51.813
Oh, I've got to fix that
back-end front-end thing.

00:31:51.813 --> 00:31:53.360
Sorry.

00:31:53.360 --> 00:31:56.002
But we can't always predict what
our scaling needs are, right?

00:31:56.002 --> 00:31:56.960
Blendle's selling this.

00:31:56.960 --> 00:31:59.251
They onboard a new publisher
with millions of visitors.

00:31:59.251 --> 00:32:01.280
They don't know how fast
it's going to take off.

00:32:01.280 --> 00:32:02.655
Or maybe they get
a sudden burst.

00:32:02.655 --> 00:32:05.420
Maybe an existing publisher
with multiple properties

00:32:05.420 --> 00:32:07.670
has tested it out on
here, and then one day

00:32:07.670 --> 00:32:10.090
says, great, let's launch it
on all of "New York Times."

00:32:10.090 --> 00:32:11.090
And just puts it-- boom.

00:32:11.090 --> 00:32:14.300
So bam, 10 times the visitors
that they didn't even

00:32:14.300 --> 00:32:15.380
tell you about, right?

00:32:15.380 --> 00:32:16.250
Because you're
selling it to them

00:32:16.250 --> 00:32:17.390
as a software as a service.

00:32:17.390 --> 00:32:19.594
You're saying, we're going
to manage this for you,

00:32:19.594 --> 00:32:21.010
and you just do
whatever you want.

00:32:21.010 --> 00:32:21.860
And so they do.

00:32:21.860 --> 00:32:22.610
They surprise you.

00:32:22.610 --> 00:32:23.500
Oh, yeah-- bam.

00:32:23.500 --> 00:32:24.750
Oh, we didn't tell you
we were going to launch

00:32:24.750 --> 00:32:26.040
to 10 million new visitors?

00:32:26.040 --> 00:32:27.200
Sorry.

00:32:27.200 --> 00:32:28.550
You want it to be able to scale.

00:32:28.550 --> 00:32:30.500
This also uses these
same core concepts.

00:32:30.500 --> 00:32:32.630
Every pod is
reporting its metrics

00:32:32.630 --> 00:32:36.530
to a central metric
system in Kubernetes.

00:32:36.530 --> 00:32:38.690
Internally, it's Heapster.

00:32:38.690 --> 00:32:42.170
And you define the
auto-scaling rule

00:32:42.170 --> 00:32:44.024
in Kubernetes for
your application.

00:32:44.024 --> 00:32:45.440
Again, your
application developers

00:32:45.440 --> 00:32:46.356
don't know about this.

00:32:46.356 --> 00:32:47.780
They don't care.

00:32:47.780 --> 00:32:49.910
Setting a target-- a
CPU is a common target.

00:32:49.910 --> 00:32:51.720
There's other ones
that you can use.

00:32:51.720 --> 00:32:55.190
Say if it's above 50% average,
give me more pods until

00:32:55.190 --> 00:32:57.180
it's-- and just run that.

00:32:57.180 --> 00:33:00.141
And then when it drops
below, mark this pod

00:33:00.141 --> 00:33:01.640
that it doesn't get
any new traffic.

00:33:01.640 --> 00:33:05.690
When it's done serving traffic,
kill it, save resources.

00:33:05.690 --> 00:33:07.260
I want to use those
for other things.

00:33:07.260 --> 00:33:12.340
So this is all built on
top of these core concepts

00:33:12.340 --> 00:33:15.009
that we talked
about in Kubernetes.

00:33:15.009 --> 00:33:16.550
Other things you
want to do with this

00:33:16.550 --> 00:33:18.860
microservice-- you need
to do canary deployments.

00:33:18.860 --> 00:33:22.610
Or you want to deploy
a new, big, red button.

00:33:22.610 --> 00:33:24.710
You want to deploy
the big blue button,

00:33:24.710 --> 00:33:27.431
or the big green button,
or the round, red button.

00:33:27.431 --> 00:33:29.180
Or you just want it
to behave differently.

00:33:29.180 --> 00:33:31.370
Probably user interface
you can handle.

00:33:31.370 --> 00:33:33.110
There's a lot of
strategies for this.

00:33:33.110 --> 00:33:35.930
Rolling upgrades,
blue-green deployments.

00:33:35.930 --> 00:33:37.040
Even just A/B testing.

00:33:37.040 --> 00:33:38.915
You want to keep them
both alive for a while.

00:33:41.090 --> 00:33:43.010
They all use essentially
the same technique

00:33:43.010 --> 00:33:45.680
built on these core concepts.

00:33:45.680 --> 00:33:48.690
You have your replica
set with your service.

00:33:48.690 --> 00:33:51.800
Your replica set says,
this is all version 1.

00:33:51.800 --> 00:33:57.824
You add a new replica set with
version 2, but the same label.

00:33:57.824 --> 00:33:59.990
The service just finds it
and groups it in together,

00:33:59.990 --> 00:34:01.550
so you're actually
running these in parallel.

00:34:01.550 --> 00:34:04.050
And you can do a rolling upgrade
this way, where you add one

00:34:04.050 --> 00:34:04.910
and remove one.

00:34:04.910 --> 00:34:06.630
It's an automatic
tooling for that.

00:34:06.630 --> 00:34:09.230
Or you can keep them both
alive and measure for a week.

00:34:09.230 --> 00:34:11.370
There's a lot of
different ways to--

00:34:11.370 --> 00:34:12.440
that's how you do it.

00:34:12.440 --> 00:34:15.290
You've just, for free--
well, not for free.

00:34:15.290 --> 00:34:17.480
For a tiny bit of attention
from your engineering

00:34:17.480 --> 00:34:19.550
or your planning
team, you have all

00:34:19.550 --> 00:34:22.670
of these things in Kubernetes.

00:34:22.670 --> 00:34:25.699
Breaking it down, what does
your single microservice

00:34:25.699 --> 00:34:27.830
look like in the
Kubernetes cluster?

00:34:27.830 --> 00:34:29.440
The replica set
is where you just

00:34:29.440 --> 00:34:31.460
specify how many you
want of how many copies

00:34:31.460 --> 00:34:32.870
of your application.

00:34:32.870 --> 00:34:35.150
The replica set reaches
out to your registry,

00:34:35.150 --> 00:34:38.960
where all your compiled
packaged containers

00:34:38.960 --> 00:34:41.870
are, says, I need
these four containers,

00:34:41.870 --> 00:34:43.280
or whatever you've defined.

00:34:43.280 --> 00:34:45.020
And it finds home for
them on the nodes--

00:34:45.020 --> 00:34:48.329
you don't care which
nodes they land on--

00:34:48.329 --> 00:34:50.360
and puts them out there.

00:34:50.360 --> 00:34:52.310
You define your
service endpoint.

00:34:52.310 --> 00:34:55.820
And now your routing traffic,
your microservice is live.

00:34:55.820 --> 00:34:57.320
A couple of little
things-- in fact,

00:34:57.320 --> 00:35:00.455
we've even boiled it down to
one command called deployments.

00:35:00.455 --> 00:35:02.330
But you can still do it
the old-fashioned way

00:35:02.330 --> 00:35:05.290
with these individual
pieces, if you like.

00:35:05.290 --> 00:35:06.619
That's it.

00:35:06.619 --> 00:35:07.660
Guy was taking a picture.

00:35:07.660 --> 00:35:08.420
That's done, OK.

00:35:11.120 --> 00:35:14.640
So that's what your single
microservice looks like.

00:35:14.640 --> 00:35:16.610
But you're a big company.

00:35:16.610 --> 00:35:19.550
You're running 134
microservices together.

00:35:19.550 --> 00:35:21.710
You don't just have a
single microservice.

00:35:21.710 --> 00:35:23.870
Your team might think
about one, but everybody's

00:35:23.870 --> 00:35:25.894
got to think about it.

00:35:25.894 --> 00:35:27.310
It's going to look
more like this.

00:35:27.310 --> 00:35:29.560
You're still scheduling
it across all those nodes.

00:35:29.560 --> 00:35:33.789
You've defined your microservice
by replica set and front-end.

00:35:33.789 --> 00:35:35.080
And it could be all this stuff.

00:35:35.080 --> 00:35:38.980
Your Go team is happy,
your Node.js team is happy.

00:35:38.980 --> 00:35:40.630
You're not arguing
about dependencies

00:35:40.630 --> 00:35:44.460
and having religious wars
about what language to use.

00:35:44.460 --> 00:35:47.004
And you can run all
these things together.

00:35:47.004 --> 00:35:48.420
There's another
thing you probably

00:35:48.420 --> 00:35:51.990
want to do as you're
doing long-term lifecycle

00:35:51.990 --> 00:35:55.292
of these microservices.

00:35:55.292 --> 00:35:57.000
There's that logging
agent as an example.

00:35:57.000 --> 00:35:58.770
That's a cross-cutting concern.

00:35:58.770 --> 00:36:00.567
We got it out of our code.

00:36:00.567 --> 00:36:02.400
Our developers don't
think about it anymore.

00:36:02.400 --> 00:36:04.320
They just write to
log, or whatever.

00:36:04.320 --> 00:36:06.450
And the logging agent picks
it up from all of them

00:36:06.450 --> 00:36:08.760
and sends it to our
shared repository.

00:36:08.760 --> 00:36:11.800
Blendle did this a
couple weeks ago.

00:36:11.800 --> 00:36:14.250
They've got a separate team
running this logging agent.

00:36:14.250 --> 00:36:15.630
Oh, it's time to make a change.

00:36:15.630 --> 00:36:17.670
We need to update that.

00:36:17.670 --> 00:36:20.250
Could be a small change,
could be a big one.

00:36:20.250 --> 00:36:23.790
In the old world, you've got
to go to all of those 60 teams

00:36:23.790 --> 00:36:26.100
and ask them, it's time to
update your logging agent.

00:36:26.100 --> 00:36:29.850
Please update this library in
Java, please update this thing.

00:36:29.850 --> 00:36:32.760
And if you're lucky,
20 of the teams

00:36:32.760 --> 00:36:36.870
have time on their backlog to do
it this week, And you get done.

00:36:36.870 --> 00:36:38.621
And 20 of the teams
are in high-priority,

00:36:38.621 --> 00:36:40.620
we're in crunch mode,
we're launching something.

00:36:40.620 --> 00:36:42.494
We'll get to it next quarter.

00:36:42.494 --> 00:36:44.160
And 20 of the teams
say, screw you guys.

00:36:44.160 --> 00:36:45.410
We like our own logging tools.

00:36:45.410 --> 00:36:46.550
We're not going to change.

00:36:46.550 --> 00:36:49.230
And so you end up with
this really-- consistency

00:36:49.230 --> 00:36:52.985
across this environment of these
types of things becomes hard.

00:36:52.985 --> 00:36:54.360
So you just went
to microservices

00:36:54.360 --> 00:36:55.680
like oh, yeah, this
is going be great.

00:36:55.680 --> 00:36:57.880
And now suddenly, you have
a consistency problem.

00:36:57.880 --> 00:36:59.790
So in the Kubernetes
landscape, how

00:36:59.790 --> 00:37:02.820
this supports your
microservices is they just

00:37:02.820 --> 00:37:05.070
say, well, look, the team
responsible for that logging

00:37:05.070 --> 00:37:07.170
agent just says,
we're going to update

00:37:07.170 --> 00:37:09.120
that container in our registry.

00:37:09.120 --> 00:37:13.380
We are going to define new pod
templates for every team that

00:37:13.380 --> 00:37:15.150
has the new logging agent.

00:37:15.150 --> 00:37:17.160
And just easily,
with just a couple

00:37:17.160 --> 00:37:18.870
of little automation
like that, they've

00:37:18.870 --> 00:37:20.932
swiped out the old logging
agent and replaced it

00:37:20.932 --> 00:37:22.140
with their new logging agent.

00:37:22.140 --> 00:37:23.806
And they didn't have
to take up the head

00:37:23.806 --> 00:37:27.000
cycles, the time of any of those
application developer teams.

00:37:27.000 --> 00:37:30.030
Those teams could focus on
what they're supposed to do--

00:37:30.030 --> 00:37:32.290
the next product,
the next feature.

00:37:32.290 --> 00:37:35.130
So that is, I think,
the story of why

00:37:35.130 --> 00:37:39.420
Kubernetes is a great place for
your resilient microservices.

00:37:39.420 --> 00:37:40.450
New stuff coming out--

00:37:40.450 --> 00:37:42.950
because we're short on time,
we won't talk so much about it.

00:37:42.950 --> 00:37:44.520
I'll give a link to it.

00:37:44.520 --> 00:37:47.420
The new stuff coming out
is Istio, a service mesh.

00:37:47.420 --> 00:37:49.170
What's a service mesh?

00:37:49.170 --> 00:37:53.010
Imagine you had to build
HTTP retries and rate

00:37:53.010 --> 00:37:57.780
limiting encryption,
injecting SSL certificates.

00:37:57.780 --> 00:37:59.580
You're a big enterprise.

00:37:59.580 --> 00:38:00.780
You're a bank or something.

00:38:00.780 --> 00:38:03.390
You need consistency
across all of these things.

00:38:03.390 --> 00:38:07.090
You can't just rely on
these cowboy developers.

00:38:07.090 --> 00:38:10.350
And anyway, you don't want
those application developers

00:38:10.350 --> 00:38:11.760
to be thinking about that.

00:38:11.760 --> 00:38:13.510
You want them just
to live there.

00:38:13.510 --> 00:38:16.230
So you need this consistency
across your whole landscape.

00:38:16.230 --> 00:38:20.280
In the same model that we had
with the logging containers,

00:38:20.280 --> 00:38:22.350
you basically--

00:38:22.350 --> 00:38:25.050
Istio solves this problem
in a consistent way

00:38:25.050 --> 00:38:29.750
across your whole space by
injecting it into Kubernetes.

00:38:29.750 --> 00:38:33.410
Istio will run anywhere.

00:38:33.410 --> 00:38:37.740
It's a partnership of IBM
and Google teaming up.

00:38:37.740 --> 00:38:39.120
Wow, it's the future.

00:38:39.120 --> 00:38:41.910
And Lyft-- this
crazy taxi startup,

00:38:41.910 --> 00:38:44.730
who supplied this great
Level 7 load balancer.

00:38:44.730 --> 00:38:46.530
So they teamed up--

00:38:46.530 --> 00:38:50.550
17 years of experience
running services.

00:38:50.550 --> 00:38:54.000
Input from hundreds of
enterprises from Google and IBM

00:38:54.000 --> 00:38:58.890
and this amazing L7
load balancer from Lyft

00:38:58.890 --> 00:39:01.660
provide this environment.

00:39:01.660 --> 00:39:04.530
Essentially, you will be
able to run it anywhere.

00:39:04.530 --> 00:39:06.990
They've launched it in
open-source method, same way

00:39:06.990 --> 00:39:10.500
that we have from Kubernetes
2 and 1/2, 3 years ago.

00:39:10.500 --> 00:39:12.240
I can see the same
momentum building up.

00:39:12.240 --> 00:39:14.700
So today, it's for the
bleeding-edge people.

00:39:14.700 --> 00:39:16.170
Six months from
now, you're going

00:39:16.170 --> 00:39:18.030
to want to run it in
your own data center.

00:39:18.030 --> 00:39:20.527
It's going to be amazing.

00:39:20.527 --> 00:39:22.860
If you want to get started,
I found a really nice little

00:39:22.860 --> 00:39:26.790
Getting Started around
Istio.io/docs/tasks.

00:39:26.790 --> 00:39:29.260
So you like this
idea in general.

00:39:29.260 --> 00:39:31.230
You want to get started on it.

00:39:31.230 --> 00:39:35.280
This is where I think you start.

00:39:35.280 --> 00:39:41.130
g.co/codelab/cloud has some
really nice Container Engine

00:39:41.130 --> 00:39:42.030
deployments.

00:39:42.030 --> 00:39:44.432
In two hours, you'll deploy
your own test microservice.

00:39:44.432 --> 00:39:46.890
Hell, two hours later, you can
deploy your own microservice

00:39:46.890 --> 00:39:48.160
using the same techniques.

00:39:48.160 --> 00:39:50.570
We've also got Code Lab
booths out there to the back.

00:39:50.570 --> 00:39:52.278
Say you're a .NET
developer, and you say,

00:39:52.278 --> 00:39:53.610
I want to try this magic.

00:39:53.610 --> 00:39:59.310
There's a whole series taking a
.NET Core from deploy, simply,

00:39:59.310 --> 00:40:02.432
to containerizing it to
putting it into Kubernetes.

00:40:02.432 --> 00:40:03.390
There's the test thing.

00:40:03.390 --> 00:40:06.730
Also, Outback training--
if your company says,

00:40:06.730 --> 00:40:08.640
we want to do this,
but we need training,

00:40:08.640 --> 00:40:10.910
a bunch of the partners
out there provide training.

00:40:10.910 --> 00:40:13.410
Or if you say, we want to do
this, but we need a jump start,

00:40:13.410 --> 00:40:17.940
we need someone to help us get
moving, there's also partners

00:40:17.940 --> 00:40:19.380
out there that
are really, really

00:40:19.380 --> 00:40:22.200
good at helping teach your teams
to do this so you can do it

00:40:22.200 --> 00:40:22.990
yourselves.

00:40:22.990 --> 00:40:24.810
And I guess that's it.

00:40:24.810 --> 00:40:25.890
Exactly on time.

00:40:25.890 --> 00:40:27.852
Or no, I'm 20 seconds over.

00:40:27.852 --> 00:40:28.560
Thanks, everyone.

00:40:28.560 --> 00:40:30.660
[APPLAUSE]

00:40:33.060 --> 00:40:35.810
[MUSIC PLAYING]

